+      <div class="block theorem">
+        <span class="block-title claim" data-grade="T">Proposition 2.7 <span class="name">(the reflected path is a Brownian motion)</span></span>
+        <p><strong>Assumptions.</strong></p>
+        <ol class="assumptions">
+          <li>\(B\) is a standard Brownian motion with continuous paths, and \(a&gt;0\).</li>
+          <li>Assumption A3 of Lesson 5 (strong Markov property), applied only to the bounded stopping times \(\tau_a\wedge t\), for each \(t&gt;0\). Each is bounded by \(t\).</li>
+          <li>Assumptions A4(b), A4(c), A4(g) and A4(h) of Lesson 5 (freezing; hitting times are stopping times; joint measurability; measurability at a stopping time bounded by a constant).</li>
+        </ol>
+        <p><strong>Statement.</strong> Assuming A3 and A4(b), A4(c), A4(g) and A4(h) of Lesson 5, define \(W_s=B_s\) for \(s\le\tau_a\) and \(W_s=2a-B_s\) for \(s&gt;\tau_a\), with \(W=B\) on \(\{\tau_a=\infty\}\). Then \(W\) has continuous paths and the same finite-dimensional distributions as \(B\), meaning the same joint laws of finitely many values (Lesson 1, Section 1). So \(W\) is a standard Brownian motion on \([0,\infty)\), which is the reflection principle of Definition 2.2.</p>
+      </div>
+
+      <div class="block proof">
+        <span class="block-title">Proof</span>
+        <p>Fix \(t&gt;0\) and let \(\rho=\tau_a\wedge t\). By A4(c), \(\tau_a\) is a stopping time, and by Proposition 2.3, \(\rho\) is a stopping time with \(\rho\le t\). So A3 applies to \(\rho\). Let \(X_r=B_{\rho+r}-B_\rho\) for \(r\ge 0\). By A3, \(X\) is a standard Brownian motion independent of \(\mathcal F_\rho\). Fix times \(s_1,\dots,s_k\in[0,t]\).</p>
+        <p><em>Step 1 (pathwise identity).</em> For \(s\in[0,t]\), \(W_s=2B_{s\wedge\rho}-B_s\). If \(\tau_a\ge t\), then \(\rho=t\), and both sides equal \(B_s\), because \(s\le t\le\tau_a\). If \(\tau_a<t\), then \(\rho=\tau_a\) and \(B_\rho=a\). For \(s\le\tau_a\) both sides equal \(B_s\). For \(\tau_a<s\le t\) both sides equal \(2a-B_s\).</p>
+        <p><em>Step 2 (splitting).</em> Put \(\theta_j=s_j\wedge\rho\), \(V_j=B_{\theta_j}\) and \(U_j=B_{s_j}-B_{\theta_j}\). Then \(B_{s_j}=V_j+U_j\), and by Step 1, \(W_{s_j}=2V_j-(V_j+U_j)=V_j-U_j\). Also \(U_j=X_{(s_j-\rho)^+}\). If \(s_j\le\rho\), both sides are zero. If \(s_j&gt;\rho\), \(U_j=B_{s_j}-B_\rho=X_{s_j-\rho}\).</p>
+        <p><em>Step 3 (measurability).</em> Each \(\theta_j\) is a stopping time, since \(\{\theta_j\le r\}=\{\rho\le r\}\cup\{s_j\le r\}\in\mathcal F_r\), and \(\theta_j\le t\). By A4(h), \(V_j\) is \(\mathcal F_{\theta_j}\)-measurable. Also \(\mathcal F_{\theta_j}\subseteq\mathcal F_\rho\), because \(\theta_j\le\rho\): for \(A\in\mathcal F_{\theta_j}\), \(A\cap\{\rho\le r\}=(A\cap\{\theta_j\le r\})\cap\{\rho\le r\}\in\mathcal F_r\). Hence \(V\) and \(\rho\) are \(\mathcal F_\rho\)-measurable, the second by A4(h).</p>
+        <p><em>Step 4 (freezing).</em> For a bounded Borel \(g\colon\R^k\to\R\), \(v\in\R^k\) and \(r\in[0,t]\), put \(\Psi(r,x)=(x_{(s_j-r)^+})_{j\le k}\) for a continuous path \(x\), and \(G_\pm(v,r)=\E[g(v\pm \Psi(r,X))]\). The function \((v,r,x)\mapsto g(v\pm \Psi(r,x))\) is bounded and jointly measurable, because \(x\) is continuous. Apply A4(b) with \(\mathcal G=\mathcal F_\rho\), \(Z=(V,\rho)\) and \(Y=X\), which is independent of \(\mathcal G\) by A3. By Step 2,</p>
+        \[
+          \E[g(W_{s_1},\dots,W_{s_k})\mid\mathcal F_\rho]=G_-(V,\rho),\qquad \E[g(B_{s_1},\dots,B_{s_k})\mid\mathcal F_\rho]=G_+(V,\rho).
+        \]
+        <p>The path \(-X\) is a standard Brownian motion (its increments are \(\Normal(0,u)\) and independent, and its paths are continuous), so it has the same law as \(X\). Since \(\Psi(r,-x)=-\Psi(r,x)\), we get \(G_-(v,r)=\E[g(v+\Psi(r,-X))]=\E[g(v+\Psi(r,X))]=G_+(v,r)\). Hence the two conditional expectations agree almost surely. Taking expectations, \(\E g(W_{s_1},\dots,W_{s_k})=\E g(B_{s_1},\dots,B_{s_k})\) for every bounded Borel \(g\), so these vectors have the same law.</p>
+        <p><em>Step 5 (conclusion).</em> The times \(s_j\in[0,t]\) and the horizon \(t\) were arbitrary, so \(W\) and \(B\) have the same finite-dimensional distributions. The paths of \(W\) are continuous. On \(\{\tau_a=\infty\}\), \(W=B\). On \(\{\tau_a<\infty\}\), \(W\) is \(B\) on \([0,\tau_a]\) and \(2a-B\) on \([\tau_a,\infty)\), and the two pieces agree at \(\tau_a\), where both equal \(a\). Finite-dimensional distributions determine the law of a process with continuous paths, so \(W\) satisfies (D1) to (D3) of Lesson 1 and is a standard Brownian motion. \(\blacksquare\)</p>
+      </div>
